+# Filesystem Authority v1 baseline
+
+## Identity and authority of this document
+
+**Version:** `filesystem-v1-experimental-20261017-r1`  
+**Decision:** **CONDITIONAL GO — Experimental v1**  
+**Base revision:** `e767a4ea70a3992c37ed604157d32f0dfbf9b1e3` (Linux 7.3.0-rc5)
+
+HEAD alone is **not** the implementation identity. The reference is that exact
+base plus the complete three-part source patch series, required formerly
+untracked files, and manifests in:
+
+`/var/tmp/authority-evidence-archive/filesystem-v1-baseline/`
+
+`VERSION.json` identifies the production-code manifest, full source manifest,
+patches and source artifacts by SHA-256. The tested production code is unchanged
+from the final qualification checkpoint; baseline/roadmap documentation was
+added afterward and is explicitly classified as publication documentation, not
+new runtime evidence. No production behavior, UAPI or class changed to freeze it.
+
+This is the authoritative **project experimental baseline**, not normative
+upstream Linux ABI documentation, a complete security system or a production
+release. Historical NO-GO checkpoints remain preserved; the final conditional
+attribution decision does not rewrite their logs or assertions.
+
+## Status vocabulary
+
+* **IMPLEMENTED:** code exists in this snapshot, not automatically fully tested.
+* **QUALIFIED:** supported by the exact recorded configuration/workload evidence.
+* **EXPERIMENTAL:** prototype interfaces/integration; no production stability ABI.
+* **PLANNED:** architecture candidate or future milestone, not usable behavior.
+* **NOT IMPLEMENTED:** no such kernel/user-facing capability is claimed.
+* **ACCEPTED LIMITATION:** an explicit boundary with justification, not a PASS
+  for an unavailable configuration or a hidden security failure.
+
+## Purpose and implemented core
+
+The project develops Linux-kernel Authority semantics without replacing Linux.
+It separates operational authority/provenance from identity, ownership and
+legacy policy while preserving independent Linux security restrictions.
+
+**IMPLEMENTED / EXPERIMENTAL:** a small static Authority Core with class-local
+operation dispatch, immutable refcounted credential-associated state, typed
+synchronous request context, positive rights and handled-operation state.
+UNHANDLED continues native Linux; handled/implemented evaluates the owning
+class; handled/unimplemented fails closed. Only the filesystem class is a
+production class. The KUnit synthetic class is test-only, not another resource
+class or userspace ABI. See architecture.md and temporal-semantics.md.
+
+## Permanent invariant and future-class requirement
+
+**A_out ⊆ A_in**
+
+Every individual transformation must be bounded by the effective, legitimately
+supplied and delegable authority at that transformation's creation point.
+Inputs must identify source provenance, explicit rights, applicable restrictions,
+cached LSM state, saved execution contexts and independently authorized grants.
+Additional authority requires a separately valid, explicitly authorized source.
+
+Legitimate delegation does **not** require attenuation: preservation is valid.
+Independent capability clones may retain all legitimately supplied rights.
+A later source reduction does not retroactively alter an already legitimate
+independent capability. Shared mutable capability state follows its documented
+sharing contract. Local attenuation, registry removal and global revocation
+are distinct. UID 0 alone cannot recreate omitted handled Authority; an actual
+held broader capability must be identified as an input, not hidden as identity.
+Independently applicable DAC/ACL, LSM, filesystem, mount, immutable-state,
+namespace, device and structural Linux restrictions remain effective according
+to the existing qualified composition contract.
+
+Every future Authority class must supply reproducible tests for this invariant,
+provenance, temporal independence, shared-state changes, transfer/inheritance,
+concurrency/linearization, and native/LSM compatibility before qualification.
+It may not invent a different security philosophy or call unimplemented policy
+UNHANDLED when the class contract says fail closed. See
+[FUTURE-REGRESSION.md](FUTURE-REGRESSION.md).
+
+## Capability lifecycle and temporal semantics
+
+| Primitive | IMPLEMENTED contract |
+|---|---|
+| F_GETFD_CAP | Observes bitmap/mode-derived rights; not a grant or a complete LSM-permission oracle |
+| F_RESTRICTFD_CAP | Monotonically attenuates mutable authority on the shared open file description |
+| F_CLONEFD_CAP | Creates an independent description/private ceiling bounded by the legitimate source, requested maximum, mode and applicable LSM checks; initial position is copied, subsequent offsets are independent |
+| dup/forked FD/SCM_RIGHTS/pidfd/fixed io_uring file | References/imports the existing description; attenuation sharing is not independent cloning |
+| Scope construction | Explicit privileged grant using pinned mount+dentry identity; currently requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN; not available after confinement |
+| Confinement/local scope reduction | Installs replacement immutable credential/Authority state; not mutation of every old holder |
+| fork/exec credentials | Retain appropriate referenced snapshots and confinement under the existing credential/exec contract |
+| io_uring personality | Explicit registration retains a legitimate complete credential including Authority; later registration captures the later reduced state |
+| unregister/ring close | Remove namespace/lifetime references; prepared requests and already acquired FDs have their own legitimate lifetimes |
+
+**QUALIFIED:** the focused personality verdict is **EXPECTED_VALID_DELEGATION**.
+Same-UID and retained-root tests distinguish actual captured Authority from
+identity-only retention. READ-only/zero handled-data snapshots cannot recreate
+WRITE or escape scope. Default submission does not implicitly use an old ring
+opener context. Forced async execution, current LSM adoption vetoes and saved
+LSM restrictions were tested. No credential rebasing or retroactive attenuation
+was introduced. Global/object-level revocation is **NOT IMPLEMENTED**.
+
+## Filesystem implementation and qualification scope
+
+**IMPLEMENTED / QUALIFIED within the recorded boundaries:** regular-file
+READ/WRITE establishment/use and bounded CREATE, DELETE, RENAME and LINK
+integration; direct-child prototype APIs plus documented VFS paths; path and FD
+truncate; supported FD attenuation/derivation and selected transfer/mmap paths.
+CREATE is not an implicit data grant. Exact-parent namespace requirements,
+source eligibility, structural errors and independent security remain distinct.
+TRAVERSE exists in bounded lookup integration but is not selectable as a fully
+mandatory handled operation by this experimental UAPI.
+
+**QUALIFIED FD implementation:** regular files on rootfs, ramfs and tmpfs,
+excluding O_PATH/backing/unsupported objects and selected async/owner state.
+Other filesystems do not acquire clone/attenuation support merely because
+ordinary path ceilings exist. Unsupported custom FD operations fail explicitly.
+
+**QUALIFIED bounded additional paths:** tmpfs idmapped ownership/DAC/ACL and
+mount-bound scopes; selected overlay permission-callback denials/allowed controls;
+generic/XFS handle acquisition and fanotify fresh descriptions; ordinary
+io_uring OPENAT/personality/fixed-file routes; established transfer/lifetime
+and source-scope versus bearer distinctions. NFS/FUSE/all callback stacks,
+arbitrary idmaps/namespace combinations, every ioctl/device and every async
+mode are not generally qualified. Metadata/anonymous storage remain documented
+legacy or out-of-path boundaries, not newly invented filesystem semantics.
+
+## Build/configuration support
+
+**QUALIFIED:** recorded x86_64 q35/KVM configurations, IA32 userspace compatibility,
+regular, KASAN/lockdep, strict KCSAN and selected LSM/policy fixtures. Full .configs,
+not fragments alone, identify actual images. Static tests use GCC 14.2.0-19/
+binutils 2.44 and the modified installed UAPI; exact tool/image/command records
+remain evidence, not a claim of bit-identical rebuilds across environments.
+
+**ACCEPTED LIMITATION:** Authority is linked unconditionally by existing obj-y
+integration. No independent feature-off Kconfig/runtime selector exists. Requested
+configuration B was BLOCKED/not selectable/not executed; pristine A and
+UNHANDLED are never renamed feature-off B. No switch was added for a test matrix.
+Stock Kconfig source is present in the base; test fragments and full qualified
+.configs select required Linux facilities. There is no missing Authority Kconfig
+patch to invent. Other architectures/native 32-bit kernels are not qualified.
+
+## LSM interoperability
+
+**QUALIFIED selected fixtures:** Landlock, enforcing SELinux and AppArmor,
+including cached-TRUNCATE no-amplification, source-context preservation, native
+assertion accounting, clone/cache/FD-use guards, temporal adoption veto and saved
+file denials, plus recorded stacked configurations. This is not every LSM,
+distribution policy, policy-reload interleaving or arbitrary stack.
+
+AppArmor io_uring denial requires a policy ABI opting into the kernel-advertised
+mediation class. The initial default-ABI fixture failure is preserved; the
+expectation/kernel checks were not weakened. Saved credentials are not automatically
+permission grants and opaque LSM blobs are not generically copied.
+
+## Evidence and gates
+
+Retained historical results, **not tests freshly executed by this freeze**:
+
+| Recorded workload | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Full regular / KASAN set, each | 536 PASS / 0 FAIL / 11 SKIP |
+| Compat32 original three suites | 42 PASS / 0 FAIL |
+| Core synthetic KUnit checks | 10/10 separately |
+| Temporal regular/KASAN/strict, each | 15 PASS / 0 FAIL / 1 SKIP |
+| Temporal pristine regular/strict, each | 2 PASS / 0 FAIL / 14 SKIP |
+| Authority/filesystem/Landlock temporal regressions | 42 PASS / 0 FAIL |
+| Temporal enforcing SELinux/AppArmor, each selected config | 3 PASS / 0 FAIL |
+| Bounded LTP per current/pristine image | 24 executable exits zero; 331 modern summary passes; legacy fcntl01 has no invented assertion count |
+
+Grouped cases, syscall assertions, executables and repeated configurations must
+not be added together. Earlier 533/0/14 and expanded 536/0/11 are different
+coverage/configuration checkpoints, not interchangeable numbers.
+
+Final gates: A/B/C/D/G **PASS**; E/F/H **ACCEPTED LIMITATION**.
+The timer cpuevt.ignore and software serial IER startup/IRQ pairs are attributed
+**UPSTREAM_EXISTING_INDEPENDENT** through source/instruction/layout/synchronization
+and historical-context analysis. Their exact reports were **not observed on
+pristine** in available logs. Finite absence is not proof, independence is not
+universal benignness, and no whole-kernel sanitizer-clean claim follows. The
+read-only final attribution checkpoint executed no new kernel workload; its
+additional intrusive campaign remained blocked/not executed. These conditions
+are retained rather than erased by freezing the reference.
+
+Evidence chain: closure/REPORT.md (historical NO-GO), temporal-authority/REPORT.md
+(personality adjudication), kcsan-closure/REPORT.md (latest CONDITIONAL GO), and
+qualification-status.md chronological ledger. Baseline evidence includes copies
+of all four required persistent archives, their hashes, reports, configs and
+reproduction materials; qualification-evidence.tar.gz contains earlier supporting
+source/build/rootfs/test dependencies. Incomplete failed archives remain outside
+this new baseline and are never credited as complete qualification.
+
+## Exact reconstruction and exclusion policy
+
+The source artifact contains the full pristine base, three complete patches,
+a ready-to-extract qualified full-source archive and a full file/mode/symlink
+manifest. Reconstruction is verified in disposable directories, not by changing
+the original worktree. New implementation headers/C files, test sources/runners
+and documentation that were untracked in Git are included explicitly.
+
+The unrelated `.gitignore` CocoIndex tooling addition is excluded from the
+qualified source and saved separately. The original worktree is untouched by
+reconstruction. Relevant generated `usr/include` exports are not smuggled in as
+source; modified UAPI and Linux headers_install regenerate them. Qualified
+.configs and retained test/build dependencies accompany the evidence. No generated
+.o, vmlinux, compiler cache, index database or repository-private metadata is a
+source input. Historical images/configs/generated artifacts remain archived.
+
+From the baseline package, in a new disposable directory:
+
+```
+sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
+python3 scripts/reconstruct.py --output /new/disposable/linux-authority-v1
+python3 scripts/verify-source.py source/expected-source.json /new/disposable/linux-authority-v1
+```
+
+Use the package README for exact build/header/test/QEMU commands and optional
+full evidence restoration. Reconstruction verifies all source files, symlink
+targets and executable bits, not just the list of changed filenames. A second
+extraction of qualified-source.tar.gz must yield the same manifest. No kernel
+installation or host reboot is part of reproduction.
+
+## Versioning and future comparisons
+
+**PROPOSED, NOT CREATED:** branch `authority/filesystem-v1` for an eventual
+review/import branch; immutable release tag
+`authority/filesystem-v1-experimental-20261017-r1` only after explicit commit/tag
+authorization and verification of the imported tree. Neither Git ref exists as
+a result of this task. HEAD alone and a mutable proposed branch are not releases.
+
+The local content-addressed/read-only snapshot plus its externally recorded
+SHA-256 manifest is the frozen reference. Read-only permissions are operational
+sealing, not protection from a privileged owner rewriting storage. Back it up
+independently; local /tmp and /var/tmp are not durable off-machine storage.
+Never update this version in place: compare a new candidate's production/tests,
+record its delta and rerun affected regression gates. Create a new version and
+qualification decision if it changes the implementation.
+
+## Non-goals and next phase
+
+No complete mediation, universal process sandbox, production-ready ABI, complete
+mandatory traversal, global revocation, no-delegation flag, Rescue Authority,
+irreversible object sealing, permission CLI or restricted native module isolation
+is implemented or inferred. Ordinary native modules remain inside the privileged
+kernel TCB; restricting untrusted native code would need separate isolation.
+The development target remains Linux, not a replacement operating-system kernel.
+
+[Next-phase roadmap](NEXT-PHASE.md) and [regression requirements](FUTURE-REGRESSION.md)
+prepare extensions using the existing central model, Linux lifecycle/LSM/Audit
+and testing infrastructure. They do not add new classes or production code.
