+# Next Authority development phase — planning only
+
+Status: **PLANNED / NOT IMPLEMENTED**. Filesystem v1 remains the frozen
+[experimental reference](FILESYSTEM-V1-BASELINE.md). This document creates no
+class, UAPI, command, grant mechanism or new security guarantee.
+
+## One model, not one philosophy per resource
+
+Every candidate uses **A_out ⊆ A_in** at each transformation's creation point.
+A design must identify its legitimate inputs, provenance and delegation source;
+independent versus shared state; local attenuation versus explicit revocation;
+issuer/recipient/inherited/saved contexts; lifetime and concurrent ordering;
+independent Linux restrictions; and absent/unhandled versus claimed-but-missing
+behavior. Existing file-capability or identity alone must not be mistaken for
+permission to operate on a different resource. Tests precede qualification.
+
+The current static Core registry, three-state dispatcher, immutable per-class
+credential state and clone/destroy hooks are useful foundations. They are not
+proof of plug-and-play multi-resource support: the only production evaluator is
+filesystem-owned, the request union currently has only a filesystem context,
+and scope storage/support checks remain filesystem-shaped. Any future extension
+must introduce only its own genuinely required typed state/context/lifecycle,
+not turn struct path into a universal object or duplicate a second policy Core.
+Changes to common representation require old-v1 regressions and a bounded review.
+
+## Candidate evaluation
+
+| Candidate | Linux integration / provenance | Lifecycle and inheritance | Independent controls / ABI / complexity |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Process Authority | Start with stable pidfd/task references and a narrowly specified operation inventory: signals, inspection/ptrace, process-handle imports and credential/context transitions are separate questions. A PID number alone is not stable provenance. | Decide child versus external-task authority, exec/credential changes, task exit/PID reuse, thread-group versus per-task state, holder delegation and snapshot lifetime. Do not revoke old independent contexts by inference. | Existing ptrace/kill/pidfd checks, user/PID namespaces, LSM and capability gates stay effective. Highest semantic sensitivity; no broad new syscall or public bitmap until actor/target/linearization is agreed. |
+| IPC Authority | Connected Unix endpoints, queues/semaphores/shared IPC handles and SCM_RIGHTS require distinct inventories. Reuse Linux endpoints/namespace IDs with stable object ownership, not a separate IPC broker inside Core. | Separate ability to use an endpoint from permission to transfer capabilities. Define sender input, receiver eligibility, shared aliases, inbound/outbound delegation, queued/in-flight messages, fork/exec and last close. | Linux DAC/IPC permissions, namespace rules, LSM/socket hooks and current descriptor transfer guards remain. Moderate-to-high testing complexity; prefer existing operations and no new ABI unless necessary. |
+| Anonymous/shared memory resources | Distinguish ordinary private heap/anonymous memory from externally supplied/shared resources: memfd, shm, externally shared mappings and cross-process memory access. Creating private computation state is not an external-authority grant. | Define handle/VMA relationship, COW versus shared pages, mmap/mprotect, sealing if already native, truncate, fork/exec and transfer, eviction/reuse and in-flight references. | Native VM/mm, memfd seals, SysV/POSIX IPC, ptrace and LSM remain. High aliasing/lifetime complexity; no global memory quota/logging/store subsystem in Core. |
+| Network Authority | Socket creation, bind/listen/accept, connect and established send/receive are distinct. Define stable socket/endpoint capability provenance, address scope and namespace identity without a new DNS or network stack. | Define accepted-socket derivation, connected-handle retention, descriptor transfer, async work, connection migration/rebinding and policy change semantics. Local reduction must not implicitly revoke every connection. | Existing socket permissions, LSM, net namespaces, capabilities and network policy remain independent. High environmental/test complexity; native loopback/netns fixtures first, not indiscriminate external network tests. |
+| Device Authority | Device acquisition, regular data I/O, specific ioctl families, mappings/DMA and control-plane effects cannot be inferred from READ/WRITE labels. Define device-specific audited scope, if any. | Stable device/open-description provenance, hotplug, driver private state, dup/transfer, mapping lifetime and asynchronous completion need explicit contracts. | Device cgroups, LSM, capabilities, driver checks and Linux subsystems remain. Highest heterogeneous implementation risk; do not extend the v1 simple-filesystem clone allowlist to devices. No native-driver isolation is implied. |
+
+For each candidate, a missing class initially remains outside the qualified
+scope and follows the existing unhandled contract. It must not be advertised as
+confined merely because a filesystem domain is active. When a class claims an
+operation, unimplemented/unknown claimed cases must fail closed according to its
+actual selected mode. No new class-wide sealing ABI is assumed.
+
+## Recommended order and rationale
+
+1. **Process Authority contract and minimum pidfd-centered feasibility slice.**
+   Process subjects, task ownership, inherited execution contexts and external
+   task references underpin cross-process delegation and later IPC/shared-memory
+   designs. The first step is the contract/invariant matrix, not wholesale control
+   of fork/exec or redesign of credentials. Constrain the first implementation to
+   a small reviewed target-operation set only after its design is approved.
+2. **IPC endpoint/transfer Authority.** Build on stable process/peer semantics and
+   existing SCM/descriptor machinery. Resolve inbound/outbound delegation before
+   claiming a no-delegation option. Keep queued and already issued capabilities
+   temporally distinct from future send/receive permission.
+3. **Externally shared/anonymous-storage resource semantics.** Coordinate with
+   IPC and process access; preserve free ordinary private computation/allocation.
+   Reuse mm/VMA/memfd lifetime mechanisms rather than adding storage to Core.
+4. **Network Authority.** Apply the same proven acquisition/established-use and
+   async/transfer model to sockets with isolated netns tests. Do not conflate
+   names/addresses with stable capability ownership or implement custom networking.
+5. **Device Authority last.** Start with explicitly audited device operations
+   only after common context/lifetime/async semantics are established. Never
+   presume ioctl/mmap/DMA effects are generic file authority.
+
+This is a dependency/risk recommendation, not authorization to implement these
+classes now. Process semantics are high risk, hence its first milestone is a
+small evidence-backed design; discovery can revise the ordering with reasons.
+
+## Next milestone: M1 process contract, no public ABI commitment
+
+Deliverables for the next authorized phase:
+
+* Inventory relevant pidfd/kill/ptrace/task/credential/exec call paths and exact
+  existing Linux checks using the same Core three-state vocabulary.
+* Specify actor, stable target, legitimate input/grant, operation, inheritance,
+  transfer and local attenuation for a deliberately minimal operation set.
+* Write the alias-versus-independent and saved-context temporal cases before
+  proposing new rights, including successful and denied native controls.
+* Define handling of task exit/PID reuse, multithreading, namespaces, concurrent
+  transfer/reduction and LSM restriction. Identify explicitly unimplemented cases.
+* Record the smallest necessary typed context/state changes and show why existing
+  Core mechanisms suffice or where they do not. Keep filesystem semantics owned
+  by filesystem code.
+* Propose tests using KUnit/kselftest and the existing isolated QEMU runner;
+  preserve the frozen v1 source/code identity and affected regression matrix.
+* Obtain design/implementation authorization before adding a production class,
+  public UAPI or any broad process-confinement claim.
+
+Acceptance is a reviewed design/provenance/invariant test plan, not a new feature
+or a passing test count obtained by shrinking the claimed contract.
+
+## Future interface vision — not current commands
+
+Illustrative **PLANNED** syntax only:
+
+```
+permission run --none -- ./program
+permission run --none --no-delegation -- ./program
+```
+
+`--none` means no initially granted external Authority. Explicit initial handles
+remain governed by their own legitimate capabilities/contracts and must be
+listed, attenuated or deliberately retained; identity or an inherited broader
+ring/broker is not silently erased by a slogan. Ordinary computation and private
+memory allocation should not require external Authority.
+
+`--no-delegation` is a separate future policy, not a synonym for an empty initial
+grant. Its inbound and outbound meaning, per-class boundaries, recipients,
+independent grants, broker requests, inherited/initial handles, queued operations
+and concurrency must be explicitly defined before implementation. It is **NOT
+IMPLEMENTED**, not a current prctl/fcntl option and not part of v1 tests.
+
+Other **PLANNED / NOT IMPLEMENTED** work includes Rescue Authority, explicit
+irreversible object sealing, richer Linux Audit reporting, TPA package-level
+permission declarations and service-manager integration. Native memfd seals or
+internal test-only sealed class state do not establish those future features.
+Restricted kernel components are long-term research only: ordinary native Linux
+modules are privileged kernel code, and meaningful restrictions need execution
+or memory isolation absent from this Core. Kernel-module restriction is not an
+active implementation goal. Linux remains the target, not a new OS kernel.
+
+## Infrastructure discipline
+
+Use Linux Audit for future security-event reporting where required; do not build
+an Authority-specific logging daemon/storage database. Use Linux Kconfig/Kbuild,
+KUnit/kselftest, namespaces, descriptors/process lifetimes and LSM hooks. Package
+and service-manager declarations belong in their existing layers; no package
+manager/process manager/network stack belongs inside Core. Keep any eventual
+ABI small, justified, versioned and fail-closed; document unsupported states.
+
+No implementation in this freeze is claimed for any item in this roadmap.
