@@ -0,0 +1,121 @@
+# Filesystem Authority v1 review matrix
+
+This is a **bounded qualification inventory**, not a new security contract.
+The accepted handled bitmap is CREATE_FILE, READ_FILE, WRITE_FILE,
+DELETE_FILE, RENAME and LINK. The production registry contains only the
+filesystem class. A missing class is UNHANDLED. Existing FDs/mappings are not
+revoked. Process, anonymous-storage, IPC and network authority remain absent.
+
+## Decision columns and independent restrictions
+
+* **L**: ordinary Linux DAC/capabilities, filesystem and LSM restrictions.
+* **U**: operation unhandled: retain L (including established additive scopes).
+  No semantic scope is required just because some *other* operation is handled.
+* **H+**: implemented, handled, matching scope: semantic permission can satisfy
+  a bounded generic DAC EACCES; independent restrictions must still allow.
+* **H-**: implemented, handled, missing/wrong scope: EACCES, even for root.
+* **X**: handled but unimplemented: EOPNOTSUPP, no legacy fallback. Exercised
+  inside the Core by its synthetic KUnit class; the current filesystem UAPI
+  rejects unsupported handled bits at entry. There is no syscall-selectable
+  X state for mkdir/stat/etc. An absent/unclaimed class is U, not X.
+
+No grant supersedes LSM denials, read-only mounts, immutable/append-only,
+sticky-bit, source-link eligibility, link count, idmap validity, filesystem
+callbacks or cross-mount restrictions. Tests below exercise only subsets of
+those independent restrictions. errno is allowed to reflect an earlier VFS
+restriction; the negative tests check exact errno for their controlled fixtures
+and verify mutation state where relevant.
+
+| Operation / path | L / U | H+ / H- | X / coverage / outstanding boundary |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| open / openat / openat2 regular existing file | normal access-mode DAC | per-operation READ/WRITE; O_RDWR needs both | Core X only; all three exercised, allowed and denied |
+| named O_CREAT | Linux initial requested FD mode | CREATE and **handled** initial READ/WRITE checked before instantiation | Regression fixes U rejecting initial FD; tested absence of denied new name |
+| O_TMPFILE | Linux initial requested FD mode | independent creation/data scopes | U regression tested; broad historical mask matrix not restored |
+| read/write/pread/pwrite | established description, LSM file_permission | managed FD bits (EBADF when missing), not new pathname checks | positive/negative data and clone/dup cases executed |
+| truncate(path) | fresh WRITE via generic provider | WRITE scope / EACCES | length checked in deny matrix; semantic positive covered narrowly |
+| ftruncate | established FD, independent inode/LSM constraints | managed WRITE ceiling / EBADF | positive and denied cases executed |
+| mkdir/rmdir, unlinkat(AT_REMOVEDIR) | L/U, no separate modeled operation | no selectable mkdir/rmdir authority | Executed successfully under all six handled operations; NOT a confinement guarantee |
+| symlink/symlinkat | L/U, no modeled creation right | no selectable SYMLINK authority | creation executed under handled mask; followed target still checked for fresh data access |
+| stat/fstat/statx | L/U and normal lookup | no metadata-visibility ceiling | exercised; readable metadata outside scopes is intentionally not claimed as denied |
+| unlink/unlinkat(non-directory) | ordinary VFS checks | exact parent DELETE | syscall matrix plus original direct capability-API tests; rmdir is separate |
+| rename/renameat2(flags=0), regular file | ordinary VFS checks | exact source AND destination RENAME | positive/negative and original direct API tests; directory/exchange semantics are not generally qualified |
+| link/linkat | ordinary source eligibility and destination VFS | exact destination LINK | direct allowed/denied destination, outside-name absence; AT_EMPTY_PATH/foreign f_cred history not rerun |
+| bounded directory traversal | L/U plus additive TRAVERSE on scoped lookups | TRAVERSE cannot be handled by this UAPI | BENEATH EXDEV, IN_ROOT rebasing, dotdot, symlink escape exercised; ordinary chdir outside scope succeeds |
+| absolute/relative symlink resolution | L/U | final resolved data object's scope matters | sibling escape denied; concurrent symlink exchange leaves outside contents unchanged |
+| bind alias / mount namespace | normal namespace and mount rules | scope uses pinned mount+dentry, not inode alone | bind alias denied; inherited handles pin old namespace mounts; userns/idmapped boundary remains unqualified |
+| dup, fork, exec, SCM_RIGHTS | share/import existing file description | attenuation shared by aliases, not receiver path reauthorization | executed; no unexpected WRITE restoration; no retroactive revocation |
+| F_RESTRICTFD_CAP | new rights = old AND requested | atomic monotonic reduction | attempted wider mask, unknown-bit rejection, parallel reductions executed |
+| F_CLONEFD_CAP | derivation from pinned source, not ambient path reopen | requested AND source snapshot AND clone mode | independent attenuation; zero-right clone; source scope drop does not revoke an FD |
+| close/reuse | normal fdtable references | no rights stored in integer fd slot | reused scope handle denied, old alias remains zero, new independent open gets its own rights, clone/close race exercised |
+| mmap/mprotect | established FD plus independent LSM hooks | managed READ/WRITE ceiling at establishment | new shared WRITE/mprotect denied after restriction; pre-existing mappings not revoked |
+| XFS handles/fanotify/io_uring exceptional fresh opens | distinct Linux privilege and open paths | existing narrow status-aware ceilings | SOURCE-REVIEWED now; historical 21-case suite unavailable; not rerun in this checkpoint |
+| metadata/ioctl/anonymous storage | operation-dependent L/U | no invented semantic policy; managed generic ioctl fails closed | memfd/SysV remain outside pathname scope; metadata, special objects not generally qualified |
+
+## Lifetime and concurrency reasoning
+
+Authority state is immutable, credential-associated and refcounted. Scope copies
+pin paths; partial-copy cleanup counts only acquired scope entries. Final
+reference destruction is deferred to workqueue context because credential free
+can occur under RCU. Scope reduction allocates and checks a subset before
+credential commit; failures leave the old credential intact. Confinement entry
+rejects a multithreaded thread group. Later reduction replaces the **calling
+task's** credentials; it is not a synchronous process-wide revocation. The
+thread test explicitly observes the unchanged peer's original scope.
+
+File capability state is attached to a referenced `struct file`, not an fd
+number. First publication uses cmpxchg; losing allocators free their temporary
+state and AND the published bitmap. Subsequent reductions are atomic AND.
+Only final file destruction frees that state. Clone takes a source-rights
+snapshot: a clone that linearized before restriction may retain that earlier
+subset. Restriction is NOT revocation of independent prior clones, aliases to
+other descriptions, or established mappings. A clone started after restriction
+has returned must not restore rights; tests assert this separately from the
+concurrent overlap window. Clone error paths unwind the new file, bitmap, and
+unused allocations before fd_install. Current fcntl FD lookup pins the source
+for the operation; close/reuse cannot reinterpret an already pinned source.
+
+Stress bounds per qualification run: two restriction workers x 2,000 calls;
+two clone workers x 2,000 calls; 2,000 post-restriction clones; 4,000 clone vs
+4,000 close/dup2 cycles; 5,000 opens vs 5,000 symlink exchanges; 128 forked
+scope-construction/confine/reduction/destruction cycles. These are bounded
+experiments, not exhaustive scheduling or allocation-failure proof. A bounded
+EBUSY retry after pthread_join accounts for clear_child_tid preceding removal
+from the kernel thread group; an earlier strict-KCSAN run exposed this fixture
+race. EBUSY while a worker is live is still asserted separately. KASAN and
+lockdep runs have no observed diagnostic; strict KCSAN findings must be reviewed
+as reported in qualification-status.md, not suppressed because functional TAP
+assertions passed.
+
+## LSM and DAC review
+
+`inode_permission_path()` prechecks handled semantic operations, retains
+superblock/immutable/idmap constraints, reuses do_inode_permission(), and only
+substitutes for generic EACCES with no custom ->permission callback. devcgroup
+and security_inode_permission remain after the positive decision. Namespace
+paths retain path/inode LSM hooks before mutation. FD I/O retains
+security_file_permission; mmap retains its own LSM hooks. Clone uses
+security_inode_permission, dentry_open (including security_file_open), and
+security_file_post_open, and retains the source open credentials. SCM_RIGHTS
+retains security_file_receive and normal Unix-socket authorization.
+
+The focused Landlock case loads an enforcing empty READ ruleset and verifies
+that a semantic READ grant cannot reopen the pathname. The earlier assertion that a source-derived clone must also fail was
+reclassified as TEST_CONTRACT_ERROR by the [targeted audit](landlock-audit.md):
+preexisting bearer authority is not a fresh pathname grant. The corrected test
+checks readable READ-only derivation, denied WRITE and unchanged pathname
+denial. Landlock file_open selects its subject from file->f_cred; clone keeps
+the source's saved opener credentials, without changing the caller's domain.
+
+A separate regression did demonstrate loss of a cached TRUNCATE denial after
+an external rename and recomputation under those saved credentials. The new
+LSM file_clone hook rejects such optional-permission amplification before FD
+publication. Original source state and ordinary LSM hooks remain unchanged;
+no opener-credential rebase or opaque security-blob copy was introduced. Upstream Landlock tests are
+additional ordinary-Linux regression coverage, not an Authority/SELinux or
+Authority/AppArmor policy matrix. SELinux is compiled in the regular build but
+no policy is loaded. AppArmor is not enabled in those configurations. Neither
+is empirically qualified here; do not infer their correctness from hook
+presence. POSIX ACL allow cannot exceed the handled ceiling; non-owner FSUID
+and mode-000 substitution are tested. Supplementary groups, rich ACL denial/
+grant combinations, idmapped mounts and enforcing stacked policies still need
+separate fixtures.
