+## 4. LINK: historical gap, current bounded mediation
+
+**Historical result:** a confined process created an `O_TMPFILE` with valid rights, then successfully called `linkat(..., AT_EMPTY_PATH)` into a different parent outside its CREATE scope. A fresh write-open at the published name was denied by handled WRITE scope; the original tmpfile FD retained bearer write authority. This established a namespace-insertion gap, not a data-write failure.
+
+**Current source change:** `FS_LINK` is right ID 7. It may be marked handled; handled LINK is required at the exact destination mount+dentry parent in `filename_linkat()` → `vfs_link_path()` → `may_create_dentry_common()` / `inode_permission_path()`. Missing authority is denied before generic DAC can grant; the operation-aware semantic grant may satisfy generic DAC `-EACCES` when no filesystem-specific `->permission` callback intervenes. Existing `security_path_link()`, `security_inode_link()`, source eligibility, mount/superblock, immutable, link-count, delegation, filesystem, and LSM rules remain in force.
+
+**Current runtime-reported bounded evidence:** `/tmp/authority-link/link-test.c` was run against the exact built QEMU image. It reported `LINK_TEST_FAILURES=0` and `LINK_TEST_EXIT=0`. Cases included: missing/right/wrong/ancestor scope; exact-parent success; rename-following parent scope and old-path replacement rejection; bind-mount alias denial; UID 0/CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE denial; generic-DAC-denied destination positive grant as UID 1000; unconfined compatibility; CREATE/DELETE/RENAME and READ/WRITE separation; LINK not granting fresh CREATE/READ/WRITE/DELETE/RENAME; ordinary `link()`, `AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW`, O_TMPFILE `AT_EMPTY_PATH`; READ, WRITE, RDWR, O_PATH and zero-right cloned source FDs; dup and same-credential SCM_RIGHTS; protected-hardlink denial; broker FD open-credential rejection; `EEXIST`, directory hardlink rejection, `EXDEV`, `EROFS`; scope drop, failed post-confinement widening, and fork/exec inheritance. The test suite is temporary under `/tmp`, not checked into this repository.
+
+**Model selected:** exact destination LINK plus an ordinarily valid source route. Pathname sources still use normal lookup and `may_linkat()`; `AT_EMPTY_PATH` uses the established FD path but retains Linux's `f_cred` / `CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH` guard. There is no separate semantic source LINK scope and no READ/WRITE data right is required merely to create the name. Different-credential broker-provided anonymous FDs therefore cannot generally be published by an unprivileged receiver through `AT_EMPTY_PATH`; SCM_RIGHTS does not bypass that normal kernel restriction. The broker can perform the link, or another ordinary source route must be available.
+
+The historical gap is closed **when LINK is handled**. A control case confirmed that LINK left unhandled remains ordinary VFS/DAC behavior, so a confined domain that does not select LINK is not protected from namespace publication. This is opt-in bounded mediation, not complete confinement.
+
+### Source-audited syscall/VFS flow
+
+```text
+link() ─┐
+        ├─> filename_linkat()
+linkat()┘      ├─ filename_lookup(source, flags)
+               │   ├─ AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW -> LOOKUP_FOLLOW
+               │   └─ AT_EMPTY_PATH -> LOOKUP_LINKAT_EMPTY + fd f_cred/CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH gate
+               ├─ filename_create(destination) -> pinned new_parent path + locked child dentry
+               ├─ same-mount check -> -EXDEV
+               ├─ may_linkat(idmap(new_parent.mnt), source)
+               ├─ security_path_link(source, new_parent, target)
+               └─ vfs_link_path(source, new_parent, target)
+                   └─ __vfs_link()
+                       ├─ may_create_dentry_common(..., new_parent, FS_LINK)
+                       │   └─ inode_permission_path(MAY_WRITE|MAY_EXEC, exact parent, LINK)
+                       ├─ same-superblock check
+                       ├─ append-only/immutable/unmapped-ID/filesystem-op/directory checks
+                       ├─ security_inode_link()
+                       ├─ source inode lock; nlink-zero/I_LINKABLE and max-link checks
+                       ├─ directory/source delegation breaks
+                       └─ filesystem ->link() -> fsnotify_link()
+```
+
+`may_linkat()` first validates source uid/gid mapping. If `fs.protected_hardlinks` is enabled, it accepts source ownership/`CAP_FOWNER` or `safe_hardlink_source()`: regular file, not setuid, not executable setgid, and generic READ|WRITE permission. The check is not replaced by LINK and is source-reviewed plus runtime-tested for a protected negative case. The idmap comes from the destination mount after `filename_linkat()` has required the same mount.
+
+`security_path_link()` remains before the VFS helper; `security_inode_link()` remains inside it before filesystem mutation. The semantic fallback is limited to generic DAC `-EACCES` with no custom `i_op->permission`; callback decisions, read-only mount `-EROFS`, immutable/unmapped-ID constraints, devcgroup, LSM, filesystem `->link`, link limits, and lease/delegation rules are not bypassed. `filename_create()` takes mount write access and the parent directory lock before the path-aware semantic check; the checked path is the pinned parent object, not a re-resolved string.
+
+The standard userspace path is path-aware. `filename_linkat()` performs source lookup, the same-mount check, and `may_linkat()` before calling `vfs_link_path()` with the pinned destination parent. That helper carries destination identity but does not itself repeat source eligibility; its only audited current caller is `filename_linkat()`, and any future direct caller must provide equivalent source/mount checks. Existing kernel callers of context-free `vfs_link()` cannot provide mount+dentry destination identity; when LINK is handled they return `-EACCES` rather than falling through. This is deliberately fail-closed, but those callers and overlay/callback stacks were not runtime-qualified. No enforcing LSM policy was loaded.
+
+## 5. Other out-of-model, unmodeled, or unqualified areas
+
+These categories should not be conflated:
+
+### Not currently represented as semantic rights / operations
+
+mkdir, rmdir, symlink creation, mknod, chmod/chown, xattrs, timestamps and other metadata mutation, EXEC authority, broad directory listing/visibility, generic ioctl semantics, full io_uring/async operation classification, and hard-link callers outside the audited `filename_linkat()` context-aware path. Some remain protected by ordinary VFS/DAC/LSM rules; “unmodeled” does not mean “known exploitable.”
+
+### Known partial implementation boundaries
+
+* Mandatory confinement cannot select TRAVERSE in the current ABI.
+* The direct `PR_CAPAUTH_RENAMEAT2` capability API only accepts regular files and flags zero. Ordinary VFS `renameat2` has parent-aware integration, but directory/exchange/nonzero-flag cases are not qualified.
+* `FS_DELETE_FILE` is a direct non-directory-entry model, not `rmdir`.
+* Namespace positive grant paths and lower context-free helpers do not all share one operation-aware decision core; CREATE over DAC denial is not uniform for every internal route.
+* LINK only covers the ordinary `filename_linkat()` syscall path with parent context. Context-free `vfs_link()` callers are fail-closed when LINK is handled; overlayfs and other callback integrations were not runtime-qualified.
+* File capabilities cover only a narrow regular-file/filesystem set. Managed rights do not revoke already-created VMAs.
+* Legacy `AUTH_SYSTEM` and global DAC override are regression scaffolding, not desired production authority.
+
+### Unqualified integrations
+
+POSIX ACL behavior beyond the focused allow/deny fixture, idmapped mounts, enforcing LSM policy, NFS/FUSE/overlayfs and other custom permission callbacks, complete mount topology/bind behavior, special/anonymous objects, delegation/lease interactions, rename/unlink race stress, all file-description operations, and all equivalent syscall/kernel entry paths.
+
+### OUT_OF_MODEL
+
+Arbitrary kernel compromise (including memory corruption that permits kernel code/data modification), malicious modules, and hardware compromise. Historical kernel-memory-corruption CVEs were classified outside filesystem authority, not contained by it.
