+## 7. Source-reviewed findings versus runtime evidence
+
+### SOURCE-REVIEWED in the present worktree
+
+* `kernel/authority.c`: dynamic bitmaps, scope pinning/matching, exact DELETE/RENAME/LINK matching, confined flag, subset/reduction, test-right sentinel.
+* `kernel/sys.c:prctl_capauth_confine()`: single-thread check, allowed handled-bit list, DAC override rejection, commit, `no_new_privs`.
+* `fs/namei.c:inode_permission_path()`: semantic precheck, superblock/immutable/idmap/callback/generic-DAC ordering, fallback limited to generic `-EACCES`, devcgroup/LSM afterward.
+* `fs/namei.c:may_o_create()`, `vfs_tmpfile()`, `dentry_create()`: separate creation routes and pre-instantiation initial access checks.
+* `fs/namei.c:filename_linkat()`, `may_linkat()`, `vfs_link_path()`, `__vfs_link()`: source lookup/eligibility, destination exact-parent LINK, path/inode LSM hooks, filesystem link callback, link-count and delegation ordering.
+* `fs/file_capability.c`: simple-filesystem allowlist, rights subset, clone via pinned path, file mode intersection, LSM checks.
+* Read/write/truncate/fallocate/ioctl/mmap integration sites exist, but existence of a hook is not universal operation coverage.
+* `fs/xfs/xfs_handle.c:xfs_open_by_handle()` is an exceptional fresh-description path without `may_open()`; its handled READ/WRITE ceiling is source-reviewed and runtime-qualified on XFS v5 in the follow-up checkpoint below. Generic `open_by_handle_at()` also passed focused XFS READ/WRITE scope tests.
+
+### READ/WRITE DAC-provider integration checkpoint
+
+**CURRENT SOURCE:** `fs/namei.c` carries a path/object/idmap/subject READ/WRITE request for existing regular-file opens and calls the existing `do_inode_permission()` / `generic_permission()` provider. Only generic `-EACCES` can be replaced by a semantic positive grant; a handled right remains a mandatory ceiling. Filesystem `i_op->permission` callbacks stay on their original fail-closed path. `vfs_truncate()` now uses the path-aware WRITE decision even when WRITE is not handled; this addresses the earlier direct-truncate gap. `ftruncate()` and established-FD I/O remain FD-capability paths. Managed-FD mmap separately enforces FD capabilities; a WRITE-less managed FD cannot create a new shared-writable mapping or regain `VM_MAYWRITE` through `mprotect()`.
