+### Authority Core follow-up (CURRENT SOURCE; CURRENT RUNTIME-REPORTED, bounded)
+
+**CURRENT SOURCE:** Every authority class stores its positive rights, handled-operation bitmap, and mode. Selective mode claims only marked operations; sealed mode claims the whole class. `authority_request_check()` has three distinct classifications: UNHANDLED returns success plus an explicit state so the caller retains legacy behavior; HANDLED+IMPLEMENTED invokes the class evaluator; HANDLED+UNIMPLEMENTED returns `-EOPNOTSUPP` without falling through. Unknown operations in a sealed class are handled-but-unimplemented. Filesystem operation IDs are internal and distinct from semantic-right names; existing filesystem UAPI bitmap positions and control structures were not changed in this phase. `FS_DAC_OVERRIDE` remains a grant, not an operation.
+
+The production registry still contains only the filesystem class, in selective mode. The current confinement UAPI cannot seal a class or claim arbitrary operations; its accepted handled operations and all existing scope/DAC semantics remain unchanged. Filesystem scopes continue to use pinned mount+dentry paths. The generic request context is a tagged union, and class operations have separate evaluation and state clone/destroy hooks; future classes can add typed contexts/state without requiring path scopes. This is a small static core, not a dynamic plugin system or universal VFS decision service. An absent class remains UNHANDLED for compatibility and lies outside this opt-in domain. No process, anonymous-storage, IPC, or network class or policy was added.
+
+Class handled/mode state is immutable under current post-confinement reduction operations. Rights and scopes can be dropped, but handled bits cannot be cleared and sealed mode cannot be opened. `authority_is_subset()` checks that reductions retain prior handled/sealed policy; `authority_cred_clear()` preserves confined state. Fork/credential duplication retains the same immutable object by reference, and exec clearing remains a no-op for a confined object. The KUnit lifecycle cases use reference duplication/clear helpers; they do not simulate a real exec transition for the synthetic test class.
+
+**CURRENT RUNTIME-REPORTED:** a clean external Debian Trixie build in `/tmp/authority-core/build` used `CONFIG_KUNIT=y`, `CONFIG_FANOTIFY=y`, `CONFIG_XFS_FS=y`, `CONFIG_IO_URING=y`, and `CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK=y`. Its Authority Core KUnit suite ran on boot and reported `AUTH_CORE_TEST_CASES=10`, `AUTH_CORE_TEST_FAILURES=0`, `AUTH_CORE_TEST_EXIT=0`; KTAP reported `authority-core` pass. Cases cover unhandled legacy-continuation classification, absent-class compatibility, handled implemented evaluation, handled-but-unimplemented denial, unknown sealed denial, selective unhandled versus explicitly handled-unimplemented, monotonic sealed-state retention, reference inheritance, and irreversible `authority_cred_clear()` behavior. The synthetic class is compiled only with KUnit; this is not a userspace test API.
