+## 11. Targeted Landlock/FD audit follow-up (2026-10-16)
+
+**Targeted verdict: COMPATIBLE_WITH_DOCUMENTED_LIMITATIONS after the fix.**
+This supersedes the interpretation of the pre-Landlock READ-clone finding in
+§10, not its historical logs/counts or the whole-prototype NO-GO. Detailed
+analysis is [landlock-audit.md](landlock-audit.md); exact raw evidence, commands,
+configs, traces and hashes are in `/tmp/authority-landlock-audit/REPORT.md`.
+
+The old test interpreted source-derived READ as fresh pathname acquisition.
+Linux/Landlock intentionally preserve existing FD access across later
+confinement, dup/F_DUPFD, fork/exec and SCM_RIGHTS. Our documented clone is
+independent bearer derivation from a pinned source, retaining saved opener
+credentials, not a current-domain pathname reopen. The assertion that it must
+be denied solely because a fresh path open is denied was TEST_CONTRACT_ERROR.
+The corrected test checks readable READ-only derivation, denied WRITE and
+unchanged fresh-open denial. It also passes on the original unpatched image.
+
+A separate **IMPLEMENTATION_DEFECT** was reproduced: source Landlock optional
+TRUNCATE denial could be lost when a helper moved the object to a location
+where saved opener credentials grant TRUNCATE. A newer task Landlock layer
+still denied fresh opens, and source/dup ftruncate remained denied, but the
+old clone recomputed permissions and truncated the object. This is real
+stored-permission amplification, not expected non-retroactive FD behavior.
+The new regression closes the client's unrestricted fixture FD before the
+policy/open sequence; the rename helper holds directory handles, not a data FD.
+
+The focused fix adds LSM `security_file_clone(source, destination)` after
+ordinary open/post-open checks and before publication. Landlock rejects a
+clone with recomputed optional rights not present in the source's cached
+rights. No source state, opener credential, Authority bit, class or UAPI is
+rewritten. No opaque LSM blob or denial/audit metadata is copied. Conservative
+rejection preserves the source ceiling and uses existing cleanup paths.
+
