+## 14. Temporal capability and io_uring adjudication (2026-10-17)
+
+**Finding classification: EXPECTED_VALID_DELEGATION.** This supersedes only
+§13's credential-personality contract ambiguity, not its recorded behavior,
+failed fixtures, concurrency findings or whole-v1 NO-GO.
+
+The governing invariant is **A_out ⊆ A_in**, evaluated at each transformation's
+creation point against all effective, legitimately supplied and delegable
+inputs, including provenance, applicable LSM restrictions and authorized grants.
+
+**A later reduction of the source's Authority does not retroactively invalidate a previously legitimate independent capability.**
+
+**A historical identity or credential snapshot is not automatically equivalent to a legitimately delegated Authority capability.**
+
+The selected explicit personality-registration contract meets the latter rule:
+`io_register_personality()` references the registrar's complete current
+credential, including immutable Authority/domain/scope state, and publishes it
+in the ring namespace. It does not merely copy UID/GID or use the ring creator's
+old privileges. Holding the credential holds Authority and pinned scopes alive.
+Source confinement/drop commits a replacement snapshot, not mutation of the
+registered one. A request resolves the ring-local ID under ring serialization,
+gets its own reference, passes LSM adoption checks, uses the selected subjective
+credential and reverts it afterward. The objective task remains confined.
+
+This is an independently valid execution-context capability despite immutable
+physical object sharing, not independent allocation of another Authority object.
+A later registration captures the new reduced state. Tests demonstrate RW,
+READ-only and zero **handled-data** scope snapshots with the same UID, plus
+outside-scope denial and retained UID 0 that cannot restore WRITE. The ring's
+mere saved opener credentials do not give default submissions old authority.
+Ring+ID sharing is explicit delegation to eligible ring holders, not a secret
+ID or a universal process sandbox. No `--no-delegation` functionality exists.
+
+FD clone and alias tests establish different contracts: clone snapshots
+source/request/mode and independent LSM ceilings into private file-cap state;
+dup/SCM_RIGHTS alias the same mutable restriction. An older independent RW clone
+retains RW after the source becomes READ, while later source clones cannot
+recover WRITE. A zero-right source clone remains zero; an earlier legitimate
+independent RW clone can itself delegate RW. A successful weaker intersection
+is not a requirement to fail the entire stronger clone request. Scoped fork
+contexts remain valid after parent COW reduction; exec preserves confinement.
+
+Source-rights reads bound overlapping FD derivation; completed reduction bounds
+later clones. Concurrent registration/confinement attempts obey the current
+multithread EBUSY guard and cause no partial confinement; entry occurs after
+the registering thread exits. A prepared IO_DRAIN request legitimately retains
+its own context after unregister, while subsequent ID lookup fails. Ring dup
+shares the registry; bounded teardown/reuse cannot resurrect old context.
+There is no global/object revocation API: that test is explicitly SKIP. Unlink,
+local attenuation, registry removal and global revocation are not conflated.
+
